        "body": "Virginia's 2026 legislative session treated data centers as an energy, water, land, and tax problem, not an AI problem.\n\nThe bill list is dominated by data center items: cost recovery for serving data center customers, retail sales and use tax exemptions, siting and site assessment standards, water use reporting, emergency generator emissions and limits, waste heat, industrial zoning, and a proposed local moratorium. AI-specific bills exist but are narrower, covering chatbots and minors, student instruction, employment decision systems, law enforcement inventories, health carrier disclosures, and an independent verification framework. The two categories barely overlap in the titles.\n\nThis matters because the binding constraints on AI scaling show up in state law as utility and land-use questions. Virginia is where that is most visible, and the volume of data center bills relative to AI bills is the signal. The four news items are all from September 2026 and center on Governor Spanberger, including an accountability framework for data centers and an AI task force.\n\nThe data does not include bill text, votes, or outcomes, so it cannot show what passed or what any of it costs. It also does not give data center counts, power draw, or water volumes in the state. The news list is four items, too thin to establish a trend on its own.",
